<ArticleLayout
  title="How to Keep Flies and Mosquitoes Out of Your Home This Spring and Summer"
  seoTitle="Keep Flies and Mosquitoes Out of Your Home"
  description="Work out whether flies and mosquitoes are following food, breeding water or an easy entry point—then fix the cause before buying another trap."
  category="Outdoor & Garden"
  date="Published August 2026"
  datePublished="2026-08-22"
  readingTime="7 minute read"
  showAffiliateDisclosure={false}
  image="/images/guides/keep-flies-mosquitoes-out-of-home.webp"
  imageAlt="A fitted insect-screen door and screened window on the verandah of an Australian weatherboard home"
  socialImage="/images/guides/keep-flies-mosquitoes-out-of-home.webp"
  socialImageAlt="A fitted insect-screen door and screened window on the verandah of an Australian weatherboard home"
>
  <p>Flies and mosquitoes may arrive at the same time of year, but they rarely need the same solution.</p>

  <p>Flies around a kitchen, bin or outdoor table are usually following food, waste or another attractant. Mosquitoes appearing around dusk are more likely to be sheltering nearby or breeding in standing water. If either insect keeps appearing beside one door or window, the screen or opening may be the real problem.</p>

  <p>Start with where and when they appear. That usually tells you more than the number caught by a trap.</p>

  <h2 id="match-the-problem-heading">Match the problem to the first place you check</h2>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="match-the-problem-heading">
    <table class="comparison-table" aria-labelledby="match-the-problem-heading">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What you notice</th>
          <th scope="col">Check here first</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Flies gather around the kitchen during the day</td>
          <td>Bins, recycling, fruit, food scraps, pet food and nearby screens</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Flies appear beside one closed window</td>
          <td>Torn mesh, loose screen edges or gaps around the frame</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Flies arrive whenever the back door opens</td>
          <td>A missing screen, a door left ajar or a screen that closes poorly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Small flies remain around a sink or drain</td>
          <td>Damp organic residue or another source requiring correct identification</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mosquitoes appear around dusk</td>
          <td>Standing water, shaded vegetation and sheltered outdoor areas</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mosquitoes enter bedrooms overnight</td>
          <td>Window screens, doors and openings used during the evening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Insects persist despite intact screens</td>
          <td>Pet doors, exhaust openings, repeated door use or an outside source</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>You do not need to identify the exact species before cleaning a bin or repairing a screen. Correct identification becomes more important when insects appear to be coming from a drain, building cavity or inaccessible water.</p>

  <h2>Check the complete screen—not just the mesh</h2>

  <p>Close the opening nearest the problem and inspect it in daylight.</p>

  <p>Look for:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>small holes or tears</li>
    <li>mesh pulling away from the frame</li>
    <li>loose rubber spline around the edge</li>
    <li>lifted corners on removable screens</li>
    <li>a bent frame</li>
    <li>worn rollers that stop a sliding screen sitting squarely</li>
    <li>gaps where a screen door meets the jamb</li>
    <li>space beneath an external door</li>
    <li>a magnetic screen that does not close completely</li>
  </ul>

  <p>From inside, bright points or lines around a closed screen can reveal gaps. Check outside as well because loose edges are not always visible from the room.</p>

  <p>A patch or flyscreen tape can cover one small tear in otherwise sound mesh. Several patches across brittle or sagging mesh are usually a sign that remeshing would be more worthwhile.</p>

  <p>If the frame is bent or the door does not close squarely, replacing the mesh alone will not fix the entry point.</p>

  <p>Renters should photograph damaged screens and report them through the usual maintenance process before making permanent alterations.</p>

  <h2>Watch how the busiest door is used</h2>

  <p>A good screen cannot help while the doorway remains open.</p>

  <p>Back doors are often left ajar during meals, gardening, washing, play and repeated trips to the bins. Watch the entrance during the busiest part of the day and look for the simple reason it stays open.</p>

  <p>It may help to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>carry several items in one trip</li>
    <li>repair or adjust a screen-door closer</li>
    <li>clean a sliding-door track</li>
    <li>move furniture blocking the door</li>
    <li>keep outdoor items on the correct side of the entrance</li>
    <li>check that a pet door closes properly</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not seal intentional drainage points, weep holes or required ventilation openings while closing insect gaps.</p>

  <h2>Remove what is attracting flies</h2>

  <p>If flies remain around one location, clean the attractant rather than spraying the surrounding air.</p>

  <h3>Bins and recycling</h3>

  <p>Check under the liner, around the rim and inside the lid. Leaked liquid and food residue can remain after the rubbish is removed.</p>

  <p>Clean the bin appropriately, let it dry and make sure the lid closes. Rinse food and drink residue from recycling where accepted by your local service.</p>

  <h3>Food and outdoor meals</h3>

  <p>Cover food until it is served and clear plates, scraps and spills after eating. Sugary drinks, meat packaging and barbecue residue deserve more attention than the entire patio.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/guides/outdoor-dining-area-setup-what-to-measure-before-buying-furniture/">outdoor dining-area guide</a> can help create a serving route that does not leave doors open or dirty dishes outside.</p>

  <h3>Pet areas</h3>

  <p>Remove uneaten wet food after the normal feeding period and clean around the bowl. Keep opened pet food in its original packaging inside a suitable closed container.</p>

  <p>The guides to <a href="/guides/pet-feeding-station-ideas-for-australian-homes/">pet-feeding stations</a> and <a href="/guides/pet-food-storage-what-to-check-before-buying-containers/">pet-food storage</a> cover these areas in more detail.</p>

  <p>Collect animal waste regularly, particularly near doors and outdoor seating.</p>

  <h2>Walk the yard after rain or watering</h2>

  <p>Mosquitoes can breed in small collections of water. After rain, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>pot saucers</li>
    <li>buckets and watering cans</li>
    <li>children’s toys</li>
    <li>wheelbarrows</li>
    <li>folded tarps and furniture covers</li>
    <li>containers stored upright</li>
    <li>blocked ground-level drains</li>
    <li>roof gutters visible safely from the ground</li>
    <li>hollows in outdoor equipment</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Empty avoidable water and store movable containers so they cannot refill.</p>

  <p>Pets still need access to clean drinking water. Clean and refill outdoor bowls regularly instead of removing them.</p>

  <p>Pools, rainwater tanks, ponds and permanent water features require maintenance suited to their design. Persistent drainage problems need a more specific solution than tipping out loose containers.</p>

  <p>Use the <a href="/guides/gutter-maintenance-guide/">gutter-maintenance guide</a> if roof drainage appears to be involved.</p>

  <h2>Make the seating area harder for mosquitoes to use</h2>

  <p>Mosquitoes often become noticeable when people sit outside around dusk. Concentrate on that occupied area rather than trying to treat the whole backyard.</p>

  <p>Check for wet pot saucers, water trapped in covers, damp storage spaces and dense vegetation immediately around the seating zone.</p>

  <p>A suitable fan can create airflow across a table or chairs, making it harder for mosquitoes to land. Aim the breeze through the occupied space while keeping suitable equipment and electrical connections away from water and walking paths.</p>

  <p>This will not remove breeding water, but it may make one defined area more comfortable.</p>

  <h2 id="product-limits-heading">Know what each product can—and cannot—fix</h2>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="product-limits-heading">
    <table class="comparison-table" aria-labelledby="product-limits-heading">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Product</th>
          <th scope="col">Potentially useful when</th>
          <th scope="col">Will not fix</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Flyscreen patch</td>
          <td>There is one small tear in sound mesh</td>
          <td>Brittle mesh, loose edges or a bent frame</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Replacement mesh</td>
          <td>The frame is sound but the mesh has deteriorated</td>
          <td>A door that does not close squarely</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Magnetic screen</td>
          <td>A frequently used doorway can take a well-fitted removable screen</td>
          <td>Uneven openings, security needs or unreliable closure</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Door seal</td>
          <td>There is a genuine gap around a closed door</td>
          <td>Insects entering while the door is open</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Outdoor fan</td>
          <td>Mosquitoes bother people in one seating area</td>
          <td>Breeding water elsewhere</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Personal repellent</td>
          <td>Additional protection from bites is needed</td>
          <td>Removing mosquitoes from the property</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Insect trap</td>
          <td>Its target insect and placement match the problem</td>
          <td>Damaged screens, food residue or standing water</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Electric zapper</td>
          <td>Limited situations involving insects attracted to light</td>
          <td>Reliable mosquito control across a yard</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Ultrasonic device</td>
          <td>No established practical role</td>
          <td>Mosquito prevention</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Choose Australian-registered personal repellents and household insecticides and follow the complete label. Avoid spraying simply because an insect has not been identified.</p>

  <h2>A ten-minute warmer-weather check</h2>

  <p>Before the next warm evening:</p>
  <ol class="feature-list">
    <li>Close the nearest screens and look for light around their edges.</li>
    <li>Confirm screen doors close completely.</li>
    <li>Wipe bin rims and remove leaked residue.</li>
    <li>Clear food, drink and pet-food spills.</li>
    <li>Walk the yard and empty avoidable standing water.</li>
    <li>Check covers, pots and outdoor storage around the seating area.</li>
    <li>Use targeted airflow if mosquitoes commonly bother that space.</li>
    <li>Keep an appropriate registered repellent available when needed.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>These steps address the most common household causes without turning the home into a pest-control project.</p>

  <h2>When the source may be beyond the property</h2>

  <p>Contact your council, local public-health unit or a licensed pest professional when mosquito activity remains unusually heavy, water is collecting in inaccessible infrastructure or the insects cannot be confidently identified.</p>

  <p>A professional should be able to explain the likely source and why the proposed treatment suits it. Broad spraying without identifying the problem may leave the original entry point, attractant or breeding water untouched.</p>

  <p>Close the route, remove the attractant and clear avoidable water first. Once you know what remains, it becomes much easier to decide whether a screen repair, fan, repellent or professional assessment will genuinely help.</p>

